C = [
("h1", "13. Clause by Clause"),
("p", "Claim: when the key clauses of the passages are set in one table, the four questions can be answered from the words alone, and the answers differ. This chapter does nothing but set out the clauses and answer the questions for each. Its use is that a reader can check every row against the text, and can see for himself whether two rows answer the same problem."),
("sub", "13.1 The clauses and the four questions"),
("ctbl", "The four questions put to each passage (part one).", [
    ("Passage", "What the Cross did", "Against what, for whom, with what result"),
    ("1 Corinthians 15:3", "Christ died for our sins, according to the scriptures", "Against sins; for “our”; the result is not stated in the verse"),
    ("Romans 3:25–26", "God set Christ forth as a propitiation through faith in his blood", "Against the need to declare God’s righteousness; for the one who believes in Jesus; God just and the justifier"),
    ("Romans 4:25", "Christ was delivered for our offences and raised for our justification", "Against offences; for “our”; justification"),
    ("Romans 5:6–11", "Christ died for the ungodly, for us while sinners; we were reconciled by the death of his Son", "Against ungodliness and enmity; for the ungodly, for us; justified, reconciled, saved from wrath"),
    ("Galatians 3:13", "Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us", "Against the curse of the law; for “us”; the blessing of Abraham on the Gentiles and the promise of the Spirit"),
    ("2 Corinthians 5:14–15", "One died for all, therefore all were dead", "Against living unto self; for all; the living live unto him who died and rose"),
    ("2 Corinthians 5:21", "God made him to be sin for us, who knew no sin", "Against our lack of righteousness; for us; that we might be made the righteousness of God in him"),
  ], [0.22, 0.40, 0.38]),
("ctbl", "The four questions put to each passage (part two).", [
    ("Passage", "What the Cross did", "Against what, for whom, with what result"),
    ("Romans 6:6", "The old man was crucified with him", "Against the body of sin; for “we”; that henceforth we should not serve sin"),
    ("Galatians 6:14", "The world is crucified to Paul and Paul to the world", "Against glorying in the flesh; for Paul; the glory is in the cross alone"),
    ("Colossians 2:14–15", "The handwriting was blotted out, taken out of the way, nailed to his cross; principalities and powers were spoiled", "Against the record that was against “us”; for “you”; forgiveness, no judging by ordinances"),
    ("Colossians 3:3", "“Ye are dead, and your life is hid with Christ in God”", "Against the things on the earth; for “ye”; a life hid with Christ"),
    ("Ephesians 1:7", "Redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of sins", "Against sins; for “we”; according to the riches of his grace"),
    ("Ephesians 2:13–16", "Made nigh by the blood; enmity abolished and slain by the cross; both reconciled in one body", "Against enmity; for both; peace, one new man"),
    ("Colossians 1:20–22", "Peace made through the blood of his cross; “you” reconciled in the body of his flesh through death", "Against alienation; for all things, and for “you”; presented holy and unblameable"),
    ("Ephesians 5:2", "Given as an offering and a sacrifice to God", "Not stated; for “us”; a sweetsmelling savour, and the pattern for a walk in love"),
  ], [0.22, 0.40, 0.38]),
("sub", "13.2 Reading the rows"),
("p", "Three things are plain from the two tables. First, the problems differ. Sins, offences, the need to declare God’s righteousness, a curse, the old man, a record, enmity, alienation: no two passages name the same one in the same words. Second, the persons differ. The passages say “we,” “us,” “our,” “you,” “the ungodly,” “all” and “both,” and the volume reports each as it stands. Third, the results differ. Justification, reconciliation, freedom from the service of sin, a blessing, a life hid with Christ, peace, being presented holy: the results are stated in each passage, and they are not the same results."),
("p", "A reader will notice that some rows share a word. “Reconciled” is in Romans 5, 2 Corinthians 5, Ephesians 2 and Colossians 1. “Blood” is in Romans 3 and 5, Ephesians 1 and 2, and Colossians 1. “For us” stands in several. The shared words do not make the sentences one. They are in different relations in different sentences, and chapter 15 says what the volume does and does not draw from the overlap."),
("sub", "13.3 A test the reader can run"),
("p", "Take the row for Galatians 3:13 and the row for Colossians 2:14. Both speak of something that stood “against” people, and both speak of its removal by the Cross. A reader may ask whether the curse of the law and the handwriting of ordinances are the same thing. The tables do not say they are, and the passages do not say so. The curse is quoted from the law; the handwriting is “of ordinances.” One is a pronouncement; the other is a document. The volume reads them as two figures and leaves any identification to the reader who can show it from the text."),
]
